25 and 74

The supplied text is Sidney’s canonical double sestina “Ye Goatherd Gods.” Its twelve six-line stanzas already obey the fixed 6-1-5-2-4-3 terminal-word rotation (mountains/valleys/forests/music/morning/evening) without deviation. The sole disruption is therefore the three-line envoi, whose documented wording must open:

These mountains witness shall, so shall these valleys;  
These forests eke, made wretched by our music;  
Our morning hymn is this, and song at evening.

Line 25 is exactly the authentic second line of that envoi; line 74 is the intruder that has displaced it. Because both lines terminate on the required end-word “music,” the mechanical skeleton survives the transposition, yet the rhetorical and syntactic continuity of both passages is broken. Restoring precisely these two lines returns every stanza and the envoi to their documented order while leaving the rotation untouched. No other pair satisfies the formal constraints and the known original simultaneously.

